---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout pageTitle="Who I Am" pageClass="who-i-am-page">
  <article class="about-content">
    <h1>Who I Am</h1>

    <div class="intro-section">
      <p class="lead">
        I'm an IT professional with a passion for cybersecurity and a journey
        that's taught me resilience, adaptability, and the power of never giving
        up.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="story">
      <h2>My Journey</h2>

      <div class="story-block">
        <h3>Where It Started</h3>
        <p>
          Nothing in my life has ever been "traditional", including the path
          that led me into tech. My route was winding, shaped by detours,
          roadblocks, and a stubborn refusal to give up on what I knew I was
          capable of.
        </p>
        <p>
          Design has always been at my core. Early on, I pursued engineering
          through community college, where I excelled in drafting and design
          courses. But life had other plans, and I found myself navigating a
          series of jobs. From my first "real jobs" working in grocery stores
          and waiting tables to my "grown-up jobs" in administrative roles and
          data entry, nothing seemed to fit. So I kept achieving and growing
          while figuring out my next move and what fit.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was through connections in those early years that I rediscovered
          technology. I was always mechanically inclined, and I'd loved
          programming as a kid on my Atari 800, so when I got the opportunity to
          help build a new computer or look at code to find errors, it felt
          familiar, like reconnecting with an old friend I'd forgotten about.
        </p>
        <p>
          It was while I was working in financial institutions that I got my
          first exposure to web development and email marketing campaigns, and
          something clicked. I took a web certification program, started
          building sites, and eventually landed at the League of Historic
          American Theatres, where I became responsible for *everything*
          tech—web redesign, email migrations, mobile apps, daily content
          updates, and social media. It was scrappy, hands-on work that taught
          me more than any classroom could.
        </p>
        <p>
          That experience showed me not only that I loved tech work but also
          that I wanted to go deeper, do more. So I pursued a full-stack web
          development certification through a university boot camp program. This
          boot camp not only honed my HTML & CSS skills but also had me diving
          into JavaScript, React, Node.js, and modern frameworks. Building
          things from scratch clicked for me—the problem-solving, the endless
          learning curve, the satisfaction of turning an idea into something
          functional and real.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-block">
        <h3>The Pivot to Cybersecurity</h3>
        <p>
          Completing the boot camp and earning my full-stack web developer
          certification didn't quite fill the void; I felt like I needed to do
          more. After leaving the League, I briefly pursued a web development
          degree, but quickly realized the program wasn't aligned with the
          technical depth I was looking for.
        </p>
        <p>
          In early 2025, I landed a role as Director of Information Technology
          at Wallace MacDonald, a green energy startup. It was the break I had
          been working toward. Suddenly I was responsible not just for building
          things, but for protecting them. Managing infrastructure, ensuring
          system security, and thinking about vulnerabilities from an
          organizational perspective opened my eyes to a different side of
          technology.
        </p>
        <p>
          That's when cybersecurity clicked for me. As part of a small startup
          with proprietary technology and product development in progress,
          security wasn't abstract — it was critical. Every vulnerable system,
          every unpatched network, every weak password policy represented real
          threats to real people, real families, real livelihoods. And it was my
          job to protect them.
        </p>
        <p>
          I realized I needed more formal education to lead effectively in this
          space. I enrolled in a cybersecurity degree program that also supports
          industry certifications. Earning my CompTIA A+ certification was a
          "coming full circle" moment for me. It wasn't just that I had earned
          the A+ certification that mattered; it was that I had finally earned
          the certification that I had set out to earn when I first tried to
          break into the tech world. I'm back on the right path by finishing
          what I started and building toward something bigger.
        </p>
        <p>
          What draws me to security is that it's the perfect marriage of my
          passion for design with my analytical problem-solving approach.
          Finding vulnerabilities, understanding attack vectors, building
          defenses—it requires the same creative, systematic thinking I've
          always used in design and development. The only real difference is
          that the stakes are higher.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-block">
        <h3>What Drives Me</h3>
        <p>I'm driven by two things: solving problems and protecting people.</p>
        <p>
          I've never taken the easy path. Throughout my life, I've faced
          obstacles that could have - should have - stopped me. Roadblocks that
          forced me to pivot, restart, and rebuild. But I learned early on that
          persistence isn't just about stubbornness; it's about believing you're
          capable of more than your circumstances suggest.
        </p>
        <p>
          I am resilient, adaptable, and refuse to accept "good enough" when
          better is possible, qualities I bring to every role. I don't merely
          want to check boxes or maintain systems; I want to understand them
          deeply, improve them, and make them more secure. I approach problems
          like a designer: What's not working? What's the root cause? What's the
          most elegant solution?
        </p>
        <p>
          As an introverted extrovert, I'm not the loudest person in the room,
          but I'm always listening, observing, and connecting dots others might
          miss. I build trust through competence and follow-through. I thrive in
          environments where I can work independently but also collaborate when
          it matters. And I take ownership—not just of my work, but of outcomes.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm also a parent, which shapes how I see my work. My kids watch how I
          handle setbacks, keep learning, and finish what I start. That matters
          to me. I want to model persistence, integrity, and the belief that
          it's never too late to chase what you're capable of achieving.
        </p>
        <p>
          At the end of the day, I'm not just building a career in
          cybersecurity; I'm working to make the digital world safer. As a
          parent watching my kids grow up in an increasingly connected world, I
          know the threats aren't abstract. They're real, evolving, and
          everywhere. I want to be part of the solution—protecting systems,
          defending data, and ensuring that the technology we all depend on is
          as secure as it needs to be. This work matters because it affects real
          people, real families, and the future we're all building together.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="story-block">
        <h3>Beyond the Code</h3>
        <p>I'm not just a technologist—I'm a storyteller.</p>
        <p>
          Outside of IT and cybersecurity, I write. I've been developing a book
          series, building worlds and characters, exploring narratives that let
          me exercise a different kind of problem-solving. World-building
          requires the same systematic thinking I use in tech—understanding how
          systems interact, what rules govern them, and how to make everything
          coherent and believable.
        </p>
        <p>
          I'm also drawn to stories in other forms. I consume anime and manga,
          not just for entertainment, but because I'm fascinated by how complex
          narratives unfold, how characters develop, and how visual storytelling
          conveys information. I play video games because they're interactive
          puzzles—systems to understand, optimize, and sometimes break in
          creative ways.
        </p>
        <p>
          These pursuits aren't separate from my technical work—they inform it.
          Storytelling, pattern recognition, systems thinking, strategic
          problem-solving—these skills transfer. Whether I'm analyzing security
          vulnerabilities, mapping network architecture, or debugging code, I'm
          drawing on the same mindset that helps me build fictional worlds or
          navigate a complex game system.
        </p>
        <p>
          I believe the best technologists aren't just technically
          proficient—they're curious, creative, and capable of seeing
          connections others miss. That's what I bring: the ability to think
          both systematically and imaginatively, to approach problems from
          unexpected angles, and to never stop asking "what if?"
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="cta-section">
      <p>Want to see what I can do?</p>
      <a href="/what-i-can-do" class="btn-primary"
        >View My Skills & Experience</a
      >
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .who-i-am-page {
    /* This class hooks into the pageClass prop for page-specific body styling */
  }

  .about-content {
    max-width: 800px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 0;
  }

  h1 {
    font-size: 2.5rem;
    color: #1a1a1a;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .intro-section {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
    padding: 2rem;
    background-color: #fff5f0; /* Warm background */
    border-radius: 12px;
    border-left: 4px solid #ff6b35; /* Warm accent color */
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: #555;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .story {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .story h2 {
    font-size: 2rem;
    color: #333;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid #ff6b35;
  }

  .story-block {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .story-block h3 {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    color: #ff6b35;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .story-block p {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
    color: #555;
  }

  .cta-section {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 3rem 2rem;
    background-color: #f8f9fa;
    border-radius: 12px;
  }

  .cta-section p {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: #666;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .btn-primary {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 1rem 2rem;
    background-color: #ff6b35;
    color: white;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: 8px;
    font-weight: 600;
    transition: all 0.3s;
  }

  .btn-primary:hover {
    background-color: #e55a28;
    transform: translateY(-2px);
  }
</style>
